Register the V2 runner's top-k/top-p sampler JIT warmups on CUDA as well as ROCm. The V2 runner calls
apply_top_k_top_p directly and never builds a TopKTopPSampler, whose __init__ is where these warmups register, so on
0.30.0 the registry stays empty ("JIT kernel warmup finished in 0.00s"), and the new split top-p kernels
(_topp_sb_stats, _topp_sb_step, _topp_sb_mask) and a _topk_topp specialization compile on the first sampled batch,
on the request path.

Measured on an RTX 3090, a cold cache volume per boot, sampled traffic at concurrency 1-16: without this, the
default profile compiles 6 kernels in-request and DFlash2 k7 11, and the first sampled batch pays ~1.86 s (cold minus
warm first TTFT, n=3). With it, 0 in-request compiles on both; the boot warmup takes 71 s (default) / 78 s (DFlash2
k7) on a cold volume and ~0.2 s on a warm one.

Backport of vllm-project/vllm@96e203b08e (#58092, Andreas Karatzas), which landed on vLLM main after v0.30.0. The
nine added lines are upstream's, unchanged, placed at the same point of 0.30.0's sampler setup. vllm-project/vllm#58465
then limited the registration to ROCm, because on CUDA every cold start pays for it (CI). This carries the registration
without that gate: with a persistent cache volume the cost is paid once per volume. On 0.31 only the gate differs.

Signed-off-by: Andreas Karatzas <akaratza@amd.com>
Signed-off-by: Michael Stufflebeam <cpuchip@gmail.com>

Kind: backport
What: registers the V2 runner's top-k/top-p sampler JIT warmups on CUDA too (vllm #58092 without
  #58465's ROCm-only gate): 0 in-request compiles, ~71-78 s of warmup once per cold cache volume,
  ~0.2 s warm
Upstream: vllm #58092 (merged after 0.30.0)
Cut-against: 0.30.0
Retires-when: the pin that carries #58092 with CUDA registration (upstream gates it to ROCm; an
  opt-in is the ask)

